# Pinned data-plane sources (rule P-17: our forks, fixed commits; a bump is a
# commit of its own). Read by CI and by docs/RELEASE.md.

# Xray: REALITY and the inner stage. v26.10.31 (the commit arngory/niko
# .github/xray-interop.env pins for the client) plus the fork's server-side
# changes (its FORK.md): removing a VLESS user through the API closes its
# live connections (revocation and expiry cut at once; internal/xrayapi
# TestRevokeCutsXrayConnections; 88b1d7b, branch raycontrol/v26.10.31);
# 5648b7a: per-user limits on new connections, policy.levels.N.limits, with
# their refusals in the stats as user>>>u5>>>limit>>>KIND, and EPERM from a
# UDP socket drops the packet instead of ending the session
# (spec/conn-limit-v1.md §3.3, part 3; branch raycontrol/v26.10.31);
# 15f4069: the same two changes moved onto upstream v26.11.9 (uTLS
# 20261108164504, REALITY 20261108101329), branch raycontrol/v26.11.9,
# which CI and the release clone and check out by this commit.
# Its go.mod asks for Go 1.27. CI and the release build it with our own Go,
# the go line of our go.mod (go1.27.4), and GOTOOLCHAIN=local: a fork that
# asks for a newer Go fails the build instead of fetching one, and the
# release checks that dist/xray was built with that Go.
XRAY_REPOSITORY="https://github.com/arngory/Xray-core.git"
# 46b58d4: one more change on v26.11.9: a unix socket file left by a killed
# process no longer fails the next start with "address already in use" (the
# exits' API sockets after restarts in a row). The head of
# raycontrol/v26.11.9.
XRAY_COMMIT="46b58d4b83a5a17b990e628fe3ffca0a240b750b"

# niko-server (front and backends): its own repository, arngory/niko-server,
# kept by the raycontrol session; releases are tagged vX.Y.Z there. v0.8.0:
# the front on UDP 53 (DNS tunnels: dnstt and masterdns backends), on TCP 443
# (tcpmux: REALITY by PROXY v2, DoH, naive and the yggdrasil backend by passed
# sockets, R2-15, R4-15) and on UDP 443 (udpmux: the Hysteria2 backends, DoH3
# and naive-quic, R3-4, R3-5; IP_PMTUDISC_PROBE, nothing fragmented); the
# Hysteria2 backends route the probe name and answer for their sessions by
# client address, and by user one by one (own, R4-20); salamander word by
# word and without cutting full packets; dnstt with dnsauth-v1 (auth,
# auth_keys_file); v0.8.2: UDP 53 with SO_REUSEADDR, beside systemd-resolved's
# stub; v0.8.3: dnstt through resolvers with an EDNS size under 1232, NODATA
# for queries the tunnel does not take, dnsprobe-v1 in the DNS front
# (dns.probe_key_file, zones[].probe); v0.9.0: SOA and NS at the apex of
# dnstt zones (zones[].ns), a resolver's retry gets the first answer's
# data; v0.10.0: dnsseal-v1 (seal, seal_key_file; zones[].seal_pad), a
# backend's answer only to a waiting query and within 1232 bytes, dnstt
# bounded under floods (a session's first packet, 512 sessions, 8 a
# device, 256 streams a session, 60 s handshakes), dnsauth and seal keys
# reread by content, masterdns from the client's fork at c9c3ab3; v0.11.0:
# masterdns sealed answers and device tags, spare dnstt zones, stats
# sockets; v0.12.0: dnsauth-v2 (auth_hint: a device's key by the hint in
# its tag, outside the scan budget), Hysteria2 streams half-closed;
# v0.13.0: slipstream zones in the DNS front (the device tag checked there);
# v0.14.0: backends.<name>.devices, the query's device to a slipstream
# server in IPC version 2 (its --max-connections-per-device); v0.14.1:
# Hysteria2 answers a TCP stream right after the dial, with the reason on
# failure (links carry niko-ack=1, internal/sub TestNikoAckPin); v0.15.0:
# Hysteria2 bytes by user (the stats socket's traffic line) and a reload
# closes removed users' connections; Yggdrasil clients isolated (a frame
# filter on each client's link, DebugPrivacy; lan-v1 §2); v0.16.0: LAN
# groups in the Yggdrasil users file, lan_relay_mbps, the Yggdrasil traffic
# line, counters of gone names pruned (render.TestLANRelayPin); v0.16.1:
# built on Go 1.26.10 (standard-library security fixes), govulncheck in CI;
# v0.17.0: Hysteria2 per-user connection limits (conn-limit-v1 part 2,
# options.conn_limit_file, limit and uptime in the traffic line;
# render.TestHysteriaConnLimitPin), journald priority on log lines; v0.17.1:
# built on Go 1.26.11 (GO-2026-6859), nothing else; v0.18.0: QUIC routing
# by connection ID behind the UDP front (udp.cid_keys_file,
# backends.*.cid_label; Hysteria2 options.cid_key_file and cid_label;
# spec/quic-cid-route-v1.md, render.NikoCIDRouteFrom), Hysteria2 validates a
# client's new path before it sends there, built on Go 1.27.4; v0.18.1:
# the UDP front against fakes and floods before a ClientHello (Initials
# under 1200 bytes dropped, pending ClientHellos bounded at about 16 MiB,
# the oldest going first), pinned flows confirmed only by the client's
# datagram to the ID the backend's first answer named (1024 a source), and
# the backends' path probes held to three times what a flow's client sent
# (no reflection to spoofed addresses); IPC and configs as in v0.18.0;
# v0.18.2: the Hysteria2 server's keepalive 28 s, not sing-quic's 10 s: a
# client that keeps alive itself (Niko's 25 s) is never pinged, a silent
# one (a phone asleep) every 28 s.
# The yggdrasil-go core comes from arngory/yggdrasil-go (0.5.15 with
# LinkDialer).
# Releases of raycontrol carry it; rayctl install puts it in
# /usr/local/libexec/raycontrol.
NIKO_SERVER_RELEASES="https://github.com/arngory/niko-server/releases"
NIKO_SERVER_TAG="v0.18.2"
NIKO_SERVER_REPOSITORY="https://github.com/arngory/niko-server.git"
NIKO_SERVER_COMMIT="960115db67d8e0d5dae773ed7c1984a7311361e1"
# SHA-256 of the release binaries: a raycontrol release ships exactly these
# (scripts/niko-server.sh checks them), under the release's own signature.
NIKO_SERVER_SHA256_AMD64="2734a47290ded1c80fee51c567d9c253a0d3c962ba166ad131f133b6eac0df7c"
NIKO_SERVER_SHA256_ARM64="8ecf5a3948305f28111929e885d7f8426c5d3e12ed51d3b4448d9c430fa39a1e"
# slipstream-server (DNS tunnel over QUIC, raycontrol docs/spec/slipstream-v1.md):
# the fork arngory/slipstream, branch server/hardening, static (musl), released
# by tag server-vX.Y.Z; scripts/slipstream-server.sh checks these hashes. The
# slipstream service runs it (v0.16.0). server-v0.2.0 (v0.17.0):
# --max-connections-per-device by the device niko-server v0.14.0 names (IPC
# version 2), places counted by the server; it and the front go together.
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_RELEASES="https://github.com/arngory/slipstream/releases"
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_TAG="server-v0.2.0"
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_REPOSITORY="https://github.com/arngory/slipstream.git"
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_COMMIT="dc5f41d3b5c5995221205ba8a0323a16ebc7d475"
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_SHA256_AMD64="59a0c3eb2a8bbbd8bdbd54c9ae8330d6e5e0dd328acab0e27d2f6f574d377b3f"
SLIPSTREAM_SERVER_SHA256_ARM64="e982d06cc11ab5a66b1f19e2715318824caa3609607a8dbb94fbd68db934224f"
# The client of the same fork (branch client/pin-alpn-sni, the client's own:
